  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1231 PM MDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nMuch uncertainty still lingers in the model guidance in regards to \nhow this incoming system will impact our region next week. Looking \nat the broad trends, two lows, one moving down from the Pacific NW, \nand one coming up from the west coast of Mexico, will interact and \npossible combine. This will give us a quickly deepening upper low, \nstrong trough, and increasing southwest winds aloft, which will \nbring in plenty of moisture from ongoing and decaying hurricane \nactivity in the Pacific. \n\nMonday will be the last quote-unquote \"transition day\", meaning that \nthe plains will remain warm and dry, while the higher terrain \ncontinues to see increasing rainfall amounts, mainly over the San \nJuans but over the rest of the Continental Divide as well. \n\nWith the latest guidance coming into slightly better agreement on at \nleast the next few days, the upper low should move through Nevada \nand towards the Four Corners on Tuesday. This will bring in colder \nair across the region, with a front moving in over our eastern \nplains later in the day. Additionally, late Tuesday into early \nWednesday will be the timeframe where precip over the mountains will \nchange over from rain to snow, though the San Juans may transition a \ntad earlier, depending on what model solution you look at. As of \nnow, snow accumulations across the peaks still remain too low to \nwarrant any highlights, but a quick few inches and some travel \nhazards will still be possible. \n\nMeanwhile, Tues-Weds, the eastern plains will finally cool off, with \nmost areas dropping into the 60s behind the cold front. Chances for \nrain showers will also increase, spreading from north to south \nthrough the latter half of the week.\n\nAfter roughly Wednesday morning, the model solutions begin to \ngreatly diverge on the path of the system. Some deterministic \nguidance immediately ejects the low to our northeast, while others \nstall it out for several days, even retrograding it west beneath a \nbroad blocking pattern into next weekend. These solutions are \nbasically opposite of one another, so most of the ensemble means \ngive a washed-out 'middle of the road' solution, which isn't a good \nrepresentation of available model guidance. Overall, next week will \nbe colder and much wetter after Tuesday, with the initial onset of \ncooler and more moist air decently well agreed upon in the data. \nThat being said, the second half of next week, namely the duration \nand departure of this incoming system, remains inconsistent, and \nmore details need to be sured up in future model runs.",
